His BP's falling.

266
00:11:39,870 --> 00:11:40,370
He's flatlining.

267
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:43,660
No, Frankie!

268
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:46,140
Doctor, do something!

269
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:47,210
Someone get her out of here.

270
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:48,870
Get me the defibrillator.

271
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:50,690
Okay, clear.

272
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:53,080
Again, clear.

273
00:11:54,850 --> 00:11:56,190
Come on, dammit, I'm not letting go of you.

274
00:11:56,490 --> 00:11:56,890
Clear.

275
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:00,100
I got a pulse.

276
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,040
Okay, what's happening?

277
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:03,380
Patient is in acute respiratory failure.

278
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:05,660
I think we're going to have to intubate.

279
00:12:05,660 --> 00:12:07,100
Alright, tube's in.

280
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:08,100
Bag him.

281
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:10,100
Somebody give him 10 cc's shot of adrenaline.

282
00:12:10,140 --> 00:12:11,860
Let's go, people, move, move!

283
00:12:18,980 --> 00:12:21,260
Doctor, oh thank God!

284
00:12:21,270 --> 00:12:22,030
How is he?

285
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:26,520
We've managed to stabilize Frankie, but he's not out of the woods yet.

286
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:28,640
He's still in critical condition.

287
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:31,440
We're moving him to intensive care, but...

288
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:34,340
Doctor, just do whatever it takes.

289
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:36,540
I just want my little Frankie to be okay.

290
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:40,240
I couldn't imagine life without my little hamster.

291
00:12:45,790 --> 00:12:46,890
Okay, Erica, so what happened?

292
00:12:47,110 --> 00:12:48,290
What emergency did you have?

293
00:12:48,750 --> 00:12:49,670
Well, it was with my cat.

294
00:12:50,070 --> 00:12:50,490
Your cat.

295
00:12:50,730 --> 00:13:04,650
Yes, I had some people over for dinner, and it was maybe 1.30 in the morning, and I went downstairs to let them out and show them where the taxis were, and then I came up and I saw that the cat was gone, and it turned out that the cat fell out of the window.

296
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:06,540
Fell out of the window.

297
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:07,260
Yeah.

298
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:11,780
Alright, but our listeners have to know, what story or what floor do you live on?

299
00:13:11,890 --> 00:13:12,650
The 20th floor.

300
00:13:12,770 --> 00:13:13,530
The 20th.

301
00:13:13,630 --> 00:13:15,710
Yeah, so this is really dangerous, right?

302
00:13:15,950 --> 00:13:16,870
That's really high up.

303
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:22,040
So I went outside and I was calling for my cat, and I was like, Mr. Finn, where are you?

304
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,200
How did you know he fell out?

305
00:13:23,400 --> 00:13:23,740
I just knew.

306
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:24,300
Yeah?

307
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:24,900
Yeah.

308
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:25,600
Mother's instinct.

309
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:26,220
I guess so.

310
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:40,860
So he was calling back to me, and so I picked him up and brought him to the house, and we had to bring him to the vet, and it was 2.30, 3 in the morning at this time, and the vet was not very happy that we woke him up out of bed.

311
00:13:40,860 --> 00:13:42,780
But that was a really good emergency, though.

312
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:43,080
Yeah.

313
00:13:43,620 --> 00:13:45,500
So anyway, we brought him to the vet.

314
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:52,710
He was in critical condition for a couple of days, and then we had to keep him in the vet's ICU for about a week.

315
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:55,020
He broke his back.

316
00:13:55,090 --> 00:13:57,050
He broke two of his legs.

317
00:13:57,890 --> 00:13:58,010
Wow.

318
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:04,190
Yeah, and so he was in the hospital for three weeks and then at home on bed rest for another three weeks.

319
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:06,540
But now he's good as new.

320
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:06,990
Good as new.

321
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:07,740
Yeah.

322
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:10,300
He used up one of his seven lives.

323
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:10,650
Nine lives.

324
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:11,180
Nine?

325
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:11,540
Nine.

326
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:12,340
Yeah.

327
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:14,300
I think maybe eight of his nine lives.

328
00:14:14,300 --> 00:14:15,290
He's only got one left.

329
00:14:15,460 --> 00:14:15,980
Yeah.

330
00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:17,040
Wow, that's an interesting story.

331
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:20,300
I guess that's the inspiration under this dialogue.

332
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:20,580
Exactly.

333
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:27,120
Sometimes we Anglos really treat our cats and our hamsters and our dogs like they're our children.

334
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:27,780
Yeah, yeah.

335
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:29,220
No, that's good.

336
00:14:29,220 --> 00:14:32,180
Well, I'm glad Mr. Finn is OK and he's walking and good as new.

337
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:33,500
Yep, good as new.

338
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:38,900
So maybe our users have some stories about their pets being taken to the hospital.

339
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:39,620
I want to hear.

340
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:40,900
Yeah, that should be interesting.

341
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:46,360
I've never had a pet actually break a leg or anything, so don't have any pet stories this time.

342
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:52,040
But we want to hear what you think, so come to EnglishPod.com, leave all your questions and comments and your stories.

343
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:53,060
Yes.

344
00:14:53,060 --> 00:14:54,540
And we'll be there to answer them.

345
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:55,780
So guys, thanks for listening.

346
00:14:55,780 --> 00:14:57,320
And until next time, goodbye.

347
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:04,934
Bye.
